WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:09.660
As I understand it, this aspect of history is not taught, and also history from the enslaved perspective is not taught enough in school.

2
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.940
And other aspects of not just slavery, as you can see.

3
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.720
I talk about desegregation and the battle of that all the way until the ’80s and ’90s.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.820
A lot of folks think that after 1865, “Oh, Lincoln freed the slaves.

5
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.360
Hallelujah.”

6
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:31.280
We had a few moments of Reconstruction, and after that, this whole Jim Crow, KKK-era intimidation.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.200
It was hell.

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.380
That’s not taught in school.

9
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.060
They kind of skim over that.

10
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.920
We took hell.

11
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.100
And I was always amazed at how my folks were able to not only buy property at the time of Plessy v. Ferguson in 1898,

12
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.760
but hold on into the time of Jim Crow, when African Americans were being lynched and everything else.

13
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.820
We’re being disenfranchised once again from this country.

14
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.160
And that lasted . . . the legacy lasted until recent times.

15
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:06.300
I think hopefully we can open up for critical research and development where we have come from and where we are going as a nation.

16
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.180
That’s what my hope is.

17
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:11.320
Whether it’s LSU, I think, in part that too.

18
00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:15.000
Other levels, higher ed, high school, elementary education.

19
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.200
So that we continue to grow and expand who we are as human beings and also citizens of these United States.
